# --- Secrets and configuration -------------------------------------------
# Config files are the likelier leak, not .env: they hold tokens, webhook URLs
# and repo credentials, and they look innocuous while doing it. Ignore them by
# default; allow-list the templates.
.env
.env.*
!.env.template
!.env.example
*.env

config.toml
config.yaml
config.yml
config.json
config.local.*
*.local.toml
*.local.yaml
*.local.yml
*.local.json
settings.toml
settings.yaml
settings.yml
secrets.*
credentials.*
# ...but source modules with those names are code, not configuration.
# `src/clawdence/ports/secrets.py` was silently excluded until this was added,
# and an ignored source file is a package that imports fine locally and is
# broken for everybody else.
!secrets.py
!credentials.py
.clawd/
.clawdence/

# Keys and certificates
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
*.gpg
*.asc

# Cloud / tool credential caches
.aws/
.gcloud/
.netrc
.npmrc
.pypirc
gha-creds-*.json

